WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.702
Strong validation starts before writing code.

2
00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.066
Define the expected data contract first.

3
00:00:05.066 --> 00:00:13.577
That contract should cover type, length, format, range, required fields,
optional fields, allowed values, nested structures, and business rules.

4
00:00:13.577 --> 00:00:20.873
Without a clear contract, validation tends to become scattered checks that
are hard to test and easy to miss during maintenance.

5
00:00:20.873 --> 00:00:22.764
Type is only part of the contract.

6
00:00:22.764 --> 00:00:26.209
A value may be an integer but still outside the allowed range.

7
00:00:26.209 --> 00:00:30.195
A string may match a pattern but be too long for storage or business use.

8
00:00:30.195 --> 00:00:35.261
A list may contain dictionaries, but each item may need required keys and
allowed values.

9
00:00:35.261 --> 00:00:41.813
Python makes nested dictionaries and lists easy to pass around, so nested
validation needs deliberate attention.

10
00:00:41.813 --> 00:00:44.650
Business rules matter as much as technical shape.

11
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.406
A date range should make sense.

12
00:00:46.406 --> 00:00:50.054
A status transition should be allowed for the current workflow.

13
00:00:50.054 --> 00:00:54.512
An account identifier should match the authenticated tenant or user
context.

14
00:00:54.512 --> 00:00:56.606
A discount value should obey policy.

15
00:00:56.606 --> 00:01:03.564
If validation only checks that fields exist, it may allow well-formed
requests that still violate the application model.

16
00:01:03.564 --> 00:01:07.144
Clear contracts make validation easier to share across teams.

17
00:01:07.144 --> 00:01:16.939
API schemas, Pydantic models, dataclasses with explicit runtime checks,
Django forms, serializer classes, and documented request models can all
express expectations.

18
00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:25.720
The best contract is visible enough for developers, testers, and reviewers
to understand what is accepted and what should be rejected before
processing.
